2026-09-10 22:21  tests/fixtures.py (new), tests/test_fixtureguard.py (new), 34 test files   [BOSS item 9, 2026-09-10 22:32]
  A TEST NEVER TOUCHES A LIVE CONTROL FILE — MADE A FLOOR INSTEAD OF A CONVENTION.
  Item 8's sweep: 12 of 35 files left at least one DELETABLE live path unredirected while driving
  the daemon. The daemon removes files under ANSWER_REQ_DIR, CLEAR_DIR, GATE_REQ_DIR, HOLDCLEAR_DIR
  and HOLD_DIR, so those tests could destroy operator state. The trigger was concrete: BOSS created
  the real hold/CODEX-1.feed at 21:52:58 and five checks in test_serverdown.py went red, because
  codex_tick reads HOLD_DIR and that fixture never redirected it — A TEST'S VERDICT DEPENDED ON WHAT
  THE OPERATOR WAS DOING, and a different operator action would have had the suite DELETE his file
  rather than merely read it. Items 6 and 7 sharpened it: apply_answer_requests and
  apply_gate_requests now run on the DEGRADED path, which is the path those tests take.
  NOT TWELVE PATCHES. Twelve patches is how the thirteenth gets missed — the same "one file instead
  of its population" shape as the pgserver census (item 4) and the route resolver (item 5), all three
  found the same evening. `fixtures.redirect_state(mod)` rebases EVERY constant that points under
  $HOME into a private temp root, creating the directories (a listdir of a missing dir is an OSError
  that several call sites swallow, and a swallowed error looks exactly like an empty directory).
  IT TAKES ONLY THE MODULE, and that is what made the change mechanical: an earlier design took the
  caller's TMP too, which would have meant discovering thirty-five different temp-variable names and
  thirty-five hand-placed calls. Taking none, it mints its own root, so the insertion point is
  identical in every file — immediately after exec_module — and each test's own later assignments
  still win, which makes the helper a FLOOR, not a ceiling.
  34 files call it; 20 took a one-line insertion, 10 needed an import anchor, 4 share a generic
  `load(mod, fname)` helper where the call is guarded on `'dispatcher.py' in fname`.
  ONE DELIBERATE OPT-OUT, AND IT IS GREPPABLE. test_restartdrop.py keeps ROSTER_PATH live, because
  its check's SUBJECT is that the daemon reads the REAL roster.json with no override — redirecting it
  would delete the check's own subject. Expressed as `keep=("ROSTER_PATH",)` rather than by poking
  the module afterwards, so `grep -rn "keep=" tests/` enumerates every deliberate live read in the
  suite and anything absent from that list is an accident. roster.json is READ by the daemon and
  never written by it.
  THE 36th FILE CANNOT SKIP IT: tests/test_fixtureguard.py (8 checks, new, wired) walks
  tests/test_*.py and fails any module that loads dispatcher.py without the helper. The criterion is
  LOADS THE MODULE (spec_from_file_location AND the literal dispatcher.py), not MENTIONS THE FILE:
  test_restartguard.py drives dispatcherctl.sh as a subprocess and holds no module object, and a
  guard with an exception list is one edit away from having two. It carries its own controls — that
  35 files were found (an empty population is not a clean one) and that a freshly loaded dispatcher.py
  really does point at 26 live paths.
  MUTATIONS MEASURED, both clean, no tracebacks:
    * a file forgets the call -> 7 passed, 1 failed, naming the file.
    * the helper stops redirecting HOLD_DIR — the 21:52 incident exactly -> 5 passed, 3 failed.
  Full suite green: 127/18/41/28/13/10/15/68/20/21/25/8/26, rc=0. Live state verified untouched
  after every run: all four hold files present, all four request directories empty.
  NO RESTART NEEDED — tests only; the daemon is unchanged.

